WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
THE STATE OF DAILYMOTION:
Remember Dailymotion? 10 to 15 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:09.400
Dailymotion was one of the leading video sharing&nbsp;
platforms in the world after YouTube of course.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.040
They were often much slower than YouTube at&nbsp;
taking down copyrighted content. They also had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.120
looser rules regarding when a frequent copyright&nbsp;
infringer would actually be taken off the platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:25.000
which made them a honeypot for cartoons, TV shows,&nbsp;
and movies. In fact, just a couple of years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:32.440
Dailymotion was fined €5.5 million for precisely&nbsp;
this and that was just one of several occasions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:38.240
But with the rise of affordable streaming services&nbsp;
like Netflix and Disney+ and the utter domination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.120
of YouTube, Dailymotion has largely fallen into&nbsp;
the shadows or atleast that’s what you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.360
think. It turns out that Dailymotion is still&nbsp;
quite popular amongst a demographic outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.360
western countries. In November of 2023, for&nbsp;
example, much of their web traffic came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.320
India, Indonesia, Egypt, and Vietnam which added&nbsp;
up to a total of Dailymotion enjoyed 582 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:08.040
visitors. To be fair, YouTube enjoyed 117 billion&nbsp;
visitors, so Dailymotion obviously does not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.360
come close to YouTube pulling in just 0.5%&nbsp;
of the traffic. With that being said though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.760
Dailymotion is still very much a giant in its own&nbsp;
right, but that’s pretty much the only thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.640
Dailymotion still has going for themselves&nbsp;
at this point. In mid 2015, a French media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.760
holding company called Vivendi bought out the vast&nbsp;
majority of Dailymotion for just $241 million. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:36.480
perspective, Google bought YouTube for over 5&nbsp;
times that price nearly 10 years earlier. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.200
it’s not like Dailymotion was late to the video&nbsp;
streaming game either. They were actually founded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:47.560
way back in March of 2005 just 30 days after&nbsp;
YouTube. But, Dailymotion never enjoyed that same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:54.720
growth or success of YouTube or even Vimeo who at&nbsp;
one point IPO’d at an $8.5 billion. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:01.440
Vimeo’s valuation has fallen 93% but they’re still&nbsp;
worth over twice as much as what Dailymotion was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:15.062
acquired for. So, here’s the tragic story of one&nbsp;
of the OG video streaming platforms: Dailymotion.

00:02:15.062 --> 00:02:16.120
HUMBLE BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.680
the story of Dailymotion dates back to a french&nbsp;
man named Benjamin Bejbaum. Ben was born on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:28.520
November 20, 1976 in Paris, France to a family&nbsp;
of doctors. Despite his academic lineage though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.320
Ben was never the biggest fan of school. In&nbsp;
fact, he would quickly develop a reputation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:39.360
of being that one lazy kid that skipped school too&nbsp;
much and got suspended. But, despite his antics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.560
he did manage to get pretty good grades and would&nbsp;
eventually graduate with honors with a degree in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.800
mathematics and information sciences. He didn’t&nbsp;
really have much interest in pursuing mathematics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.640
as a career path though. In fact, throughout his&nbsp;
college years, he had actually picked up a habit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.760
of late night gaming which quickly turned into Ben&nbsp;
teaching himself how to code and creating his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.800
websites and games. But, while he enjoyed pursuing&nbsp;
these projects, they didn’t exactly pay the bills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.120
so he would end up picking up a bunch of&nbsp;
odd jobs throughout the 90s to get by. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.880
included helping tourists, being a telemarketer,&nbsp;
and working in the telecom industry. Feeling lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:21.800
Ben would eventually return to college when he&nbsp;
was approached by a cafe manager. The manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.360
was looking to put together a website for his cafe&nbsp;
and felt that Ben was the perfect guy to do it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.280
that’s when everything clicked for Ben. What he&nbsp;
needed to do was create a web design studio and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.360
help local businesses create their own websites.&nbsp;
He would team up with one of his cousins, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:43.520
together, they would create Iguana Studio in 2000.&nbsp;
Over the next couple of years, Iguana Studio would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.360
evolve into a decent personal business allowing&nbsp;
Ben to take a vacation to New York in 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.640
and it was here that the idea for Dailymotion was&nbsp;
born. You see, Ben had taken some videos of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:00.000
snowfall in Manhattan and wanted to share it with&nbsp;
his friends and family back in Paris. But to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.480
surprise, there was no easy way to share large&nbsp;
video files over the internet. So, as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:10.640
he returned home, he would team up with one of his&nbsp;
friends, Olivier Poitrey and whip up a simple site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:17.200
to share videos online called short.tv. Initially,&nbsp;
this was much more of a public service as opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.400
to a serious business but to the duo’s surprise,&nbsp;
the website would quickly pick up quite a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.320
momentum within France. Short.tv was very much&nbsp;
just something that these to were running out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.800
of Poitrey’s apartment. So, to accommodate for&nbsp;
this influx in traffic, they would raise a modest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:40.080
€6,000 from 6 people, and they would rename the&nbsp;
website Dailymotion. Dailymotion never experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.360
the scale of growth that YouTube enjoyed but they&nbsp;
were growing pretty fast within France. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.320
just 18 months after being founded,&nbsp;
Dailymotion would lock in the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:56.120
French Web 2.0 fundraising round of 2006 worth&nbsp;
7 million euros. Just a few months after this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:02.000
Google would buy YouTube for $1.65 billion which&nbsp;
pushed Dailymotion’s investors to double down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.960
their bet. In 2007, they would provide Dailymotion&nbsp;
with another 25 million euros in funding to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.680
their hand at global expansion. But while they had&nbsp;
access to a large amount of capital, the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.480
is that no amount of money is every enough in&nbsp;
the world of free online video streaming. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:29.480
the Dailymotion team would learn this first&nbsp;
hand as they faced challenge after challenge.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.400
OBSTACLE AFTER OBSTACLE:
With little to no content moderation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:39.240
Dailymotion would almost immediately be dragged&nbsp;
into the world of copyright litigation. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.520
just two years after founding the company,&nbsp;
Dailymotion would get bogged down with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:49.120
first copyright infringement lawsuit. They tried&nbsp;
to argue that they were just a content platform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:55.480
not an active infringer of copyright regulation,&nbsp;
but this argument didn’t hold up in court. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.160
court ruled that Dailymotion was very much aware&nbsp;
of copyrighted content being uploaded onto their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.800
website, and as such, it was up to them to&nbsp;
remove it in a timely manner. Not exactly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:11.520
ruling they were looking for but it’s not like&nbsp;
they had the time to appeal. Every single day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.160
Dailymotion was burning boatloads of money keeping&nbsp;
the website up and running for millions of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:22.320
to use for free. So, their number one priority was&nbsp;
to get monetization up and running so that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:28.960
could atleast off set some of the losses. However,&nbsp;
this was a lot easier said than done. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.120
in the late 2000s, instream video ads like the&nbsp;
one you probably saw before this video weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:39.800
even a thing. All you had were the static display&nbsp;
ads that you saw on websites. Advertisers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:45.480
also rather skeptical about digital advertising in&nbsp;
general. So, Dailymotion was stuck showing display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:50.760
ads on a video platform which as you would&nbsp;
guess was not all that lucrative. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:56.280
Dailymotion was quickly back to the fundraising&nbsp;
table in 2009. The good news though was that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.880
were able to get some pretty reputable backers&nbsp;
such as the French government themselves who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.560
invested 17 million into the platform. Just as&nbsp;
Dailymotion was getting back on their feet though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.000
they would be hit with not just another&nbsp;
lawsuit but an outright ban from India in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:16.840
May of 2012. Fortunately, India would roll back&nbsp;
this ruling in the following months citing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:22.600
only certain URLs should be blocked for copyright&nbsp;
infringement, not entire websites. This event made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:28.160
Dailymotion realize that they not only needed&nbsp;
allies within France but around the world. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.200
they would form partnerships with several&nbsp;
international media publications including Hearst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:38.720
Media and Bloomberg. They would also open offices&nbsp;
in London, San Francisco, and Singapore. This may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:46.200
have given them a better reputation but it didn’t&nbsp;
exactly stop the lawsuits. In fact, their home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:52.360
city would fine them for 1.3 million euros in late&nbsp;
2014. This was followed by another temporary ban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:58.880
in India and a permanent ban in Russia. Throughout&nbsp;
this entire time period, Dailymotion was basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:03.960
just juggling 3 things over and over again. They&nbsp;
were addressing copyright infringement lawsuits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:08.720
trying to raise money, and then solving issues&nbsp;
with scale. After that, they would once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:14.360
address copyright infringement lawsuits, raise&nbsp;
capital, and solve issues with scale. This vicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:21.000
cycle repeated over and over again up until&nbsp;
2017. By this point, streaming services were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.800
really taking off so most copyright holders&nbsp;
turned their focus to monetizing streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:31.680
services instead of going after Dailymotion. But,&nbsp;
while this finally gave Dailymotion a break, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:44.424
was also accompanied by a large outflow of users&nbsp;
which brings us into the modern day Dailymotion.

00:08:44.424 --> 00:08:46.000
THE TRAGIC FATE:
Honestly, I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:52.000
Dailymotion ever really wanted to be some sort&nbsp;
of piracy streaming center. Other sites like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.840
Megaupload very much leaned into this persona&nbsp;
and used it their benefit to maximize profits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:02.440
Dailymotion on the other hand was just truly just&nbsp;
trying to create a competitor to YouTube but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:08.520
just so happened that piracy is what it’s users&nbsp;
often turned to Dailymotion for. Much of this just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:14.280
had to do with the immense resources that YouTube&nbsp;
had thanks to Google. They had the advertisers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:19.000
the ability to stomach insane losses, the&nbsp;
Google ecosystem, massive infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:25.240
and the ability to payout creators. As such,&nbsp;
YouTube was the clear choice for regular videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:30.560
and Dailymotion naturally become a place that&nbsp;
hosted content that was not available on YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.960
which mainly consistent of copyrighted content.&nbsp;
This put Dailymotion in a really tricky situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:42.280
Do they turn a blind eye to copyrighted content to&nbsp;
pump up their user base or do they continue with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:47.960
their nobel efforts. It seems that Dailymotion&nbsp;
was stuck in a constant limbo between these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:54.320
efforts as they fought one lawsuit after another.&nbsp;
Eventually, a decision was forced upon them when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.320 --> 00:10:00.480
streaming took off. With affordable streaming&nbsp;
services, most people simply had no reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.840
use an off brand version of YouTube anymore. As&nbsp;
such, the interest in Dailymotion slowly bled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:11.360
out throughout the second half of the 2010s.&nbsp;
It seems that the founders somewhat expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.040
this as they very much made decisions that would&nbsp;
protect themselves from this outcome. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:22.720
Ben was already taking steps out the door by mid&nbsp;
2008 when he stepped down from being CEO. And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:27.200
would completely cash out of the company&nbsp;
at roughly a 120 million euro valuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:33.000
in the early 2010s. It’s not clear how much&nbsp;
of the company he still owned at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:39.200
but if it was any sizable amount, he likely walked&nbsp;
away with tens of millions. Since then, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.920
that Ben has circled all the way back to academics&nbsp;
as he is now a professor of Python at the Albert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:47.000
School in Paris. As for Olivier, he stuck around&nbsp;
at Dailymotion till mid 2016 as CTO. After that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:51.960
Olivier would score a job at Netflix as&nbsp;
director of engineer. And if you didn’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:57.800
Netflix pays $1.2 million cash for directors&nbsp;
of software engineering. So, I think it’s safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:03.120
to say that the founders are fortunately doing&nbsp;
better than ever. The same, however, cannot be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:09.560
said about Dailymotion. Over the years, there were&nbsp;
some brief moments of great hope for the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:15.080
For example, in 2014, Microsoft nearly invested&nbsp;
in Dailymotion, something that may have taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:17.600
the platform to a wholenother level. But, this&nbsp;
deal unfortunately never came to fruition. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:23.920
did end up happening was Vivendi buying out&nbsp;
Dailymotion for roughly $300 million. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:29.200
Dailymotion has really just been running on&nbsp;
fumes. The main reason that they’re still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:35.320
somewhat popular today is largely just because&nbsp;
they used to be popular. And with Dailymotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:39.560
making decisions like removing the comment&nbsp;
section and even the view counter altogether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:44.440
largely because their videos weren’t getting any&nbsp;
views, things don’t look so bright for Dailymotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.600
moving forward. Best case scenario, it&nbsp;
seems that Dailymotion will just be be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:51.720
tossed around from one owner to another&nbsp;
just like AOL or Yahoo while it still has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:57.720
some juice left. Eventually though, that juice&nbsp;
will run out at which point the platform will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:03.600
be completely shutdown and that is the&nbsp;
tragic fate of Dailymotion. Another OG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:08.560
video platform that’s facing a similar fate&nbsp;
is Vimeo. Check out this video to learn more.

